candidates.comparison_that_would_overturn:
Build a preregistered mutation set on a copy of the blueprint backend. Each mutation breaks the response contract:
- drop or rename a response_model field;
- return an undeclared status code;
- raise a 500 on a schema-valid edge input;
- make PATCH accept an invalid body.

Run test_api.py, the frozen browser/ledger.spec.ts and `schemathesis run` against the app's /openapi.json.

Promote to targeted_candidate if Schemathesis catches at least one contract mutation that both existing oracles miss, retains the failing case so it can be replayed, and reports zero failures on the unmutated app (negative control). Keep it parked if it finds only what test_api.py already finds, or if it flags the clean app.

Run this only after confirming a UPS is attached. Without one, record power loss as an accepted residual in GAP0. Recovery then rests on the BIOS restoring AC power, an at-startup task, ext4 journal replay inside the VHDX, and Dagu's zombie detector and catch-up.

With a UPS, cut mains 3 times while a DAG step traps SIGTERM and writes fsync'd timestamps.
- Arm A: Windows-native HID UPS battery, with the critical battery action (PowerCfg BATACTIONCRIT, GUID <session-id>) set to Shut Down.
- Arm B: NUT for Windows 2.8.5 upsmon, whose SHUTDOWNCMD first terminates the distro and then shuts Windows down.

Measure:
- the time from going on battery to Windows shutdown;
- the last timestamp written;
- whether Dagu logs 'All services stopped gracefully' in journalctl --user -b -1;
- the run's status after power returns.

Adopt NUT only if Windows cannot see the UPS as a HID battery (a network or SNMP UPS, or one UPS shared by several hosts), or if arm B brings the in-flight run to a terminal status where arm A does not.

Run once a LAN-client, remote-service or production-authentication requirement is recorded. Bounded trial on linux-wsl2, no account:
- Install the v2.11.7 release archive after verifying the .sig/.pem signature files on its checksums file.
- Put one reverse_proxy site with tls internal in front of the Next.js origin (127.0.0.1:18080; the API rides its rewrite). Configure it for localhost and one LAN name (.home.arpa or .internal), changing the fixture's bind only as that requirement dictates.
- Install the 'Caddy Local Authority' root on each client.
- Rerun the unchanged 12 API checks and the Playwright create/update flow over HTTPS from Linux Playwright, a Windows browser and one Mac.
Measure:
- pass rate, which must stay 12/12 plus the browser flow;
- handshake result per client;
- added p50/p95 latency against the direct path at a fixed load from the selected generator (Locust, if adopted);
- a streaming response held open past 60 s;
- intermediate renewal without a restart;
- rollback: caddy untrust, stop, direct port restored.
Caddy loses if a check fails only through the proxy, if the added p95 exceeds a budget fixed before the run (proposed: 10%), or if Traefik v3.7.14 with file-provider certificates from a local CA passes the same matrix with no more root-distribution or configuration steps.

Only once a remote-service requirement is recorded. Run headscale v0.29.4 on a publicly reachable host, with the Tailscale client on the workstation and one Mac. Measure:
- reachability of the fixture from off the LAN;
- the unchanged 12 API checks and browser flow over the tailnet;
- added latency;
- behaviour of existing peers across a control-server restart;
- the public port footprint.
Compare against the same requirement served by Tailscale's hosted coordination server (new account) or by a plain WireGuard tunnel. Drop headscale if the requirement is met with no public control host, or if an alternative passes with less exposed surface.

When the trigger fires, run Traefik and Caddy head to head on the same matrix. Traefik v3.7.14 uses file-provider certificates from a local CA for the same localhost and LAN names. Measure:
- the unchanged 12 API checks and browser flow from Linux, Windows and Mac clients;
- the number of root-distribution and configuration steps;
- the p95 latency added at a fixed load;
- streaming held open past 60 s;
- rollback.
Traefik becomes the targeted choice in either case:
- a container lane with label routing (Compose or Podman) is selected, and Traefik passes with no more steps than Caddy;
- a public domain with ACME is chosen, which matches the FastAPI vendor template.

Only after the Granite FP8 trial adopts a vLLM generation route. On the same pinned vLLM and request set, add arm D: an llm-compressor FP8_DYNAMIC or GPTQ W4A16 checkpoint, made only for a model that has no vendor or RedHatAI artifact, or RedHatAI/Qwen3.8-27B-INT4 against the production llama.cpp Qwen3.8-27B GGUF. Compare it with online fp8_per_tensor and the vendor artifact on:
- lm-evaluation-harness and promptfoo fidelity against BF16, or against the GGUF control for 27B;
- peak VRAM, and whether the model runs with no CPU offload;
- TTFT and ITL from vLLM /metrics in the existing Prometheus;
- restart-to-first-token (offline artifact vs load-time quantization).
It is adopted only if it fits a model the other arms cannot, or beats them on fidelity at equal VRAM and latency.

